#
# =========================================================================================
# TWO PROCESS FAILURES FIRST, BECAUSE THE RESULT IS GOOD AND THAT IS WHEN THEY GET BURIED
# =========================================================================================
#
# **1. THE FREE-SPACE PRECONDITION FIRED AND THE RUN PROCEEDED ANYWAY.**
#
#   /home/revanth-aditya/delphi-target   1089M   floor 2048M   *** SHORT ***
#   "SHORT ON SPACE ... This is a PRECONDITION failure, not a build or test failure"
#
# The guard did exactly what it was built for and its refusal was overridden. Nothing
# observable went wrong — 17/17 producers exited 0, freshness proved, counts matched — so this
# is NOT a claim that the evidence is tainted. It is the record that the run STARTED FROM A
# STATE THE PROCEDURE REFUSES, and the whole point of that guard (D0A-R29-08) is that its
# failure mode surfaces far away wearing someone else's name. A run that begins by overriding
# its own precondition has spent the guard's value whether or not it got away with it.
# **Free space before run 21.** Part B's feature build consumed more still.
#
# **2. MY HANDOVER DROPPED §4b, THE MEMBERSHIP CHECK — AND ITS INPUTS WERE NOT RETAINED.**
#
# D0A-R33 ADDENDUM: the reviewer could not independently verify the 227/227 result, because
# `/tmp/root-run20.log` and `/tmp/targets.txt` are both gone. So the membership claim in this
# record **stands on my report of having run it, not on evidence anyone else can check** — and
# matching totals cannot substitute, which is the whole reason §4b exists. That is the second
# edge of "closed-after is not run": the check was performed late AND its inputs were transient,
# so lateness became unverifiability. A future §4b must write its identity lists into the run's
# retained artifacts, not leave them in /tmp.
#
# I assembled the operator's command sequence from the procedure and omitted the identity
# membership check — the step that exists because equal totals are not equal test sets
# (D0A-R27-04). Run 19 carried it; run 20's sequence did not.
#
# **This is the defect that got `scripts/lane-run.sh` REVERTED at D0A-R23-05** — a runner that
# dropped checks the procedure performs, sold as an upgrade. I did the same thing by hand, one
# check instead of five, in the very session that quoted that reversion. It was closed after
# the fact (the log and target list were still on disk; 227/227, zero either direction, rc 0)
# and the result is sound — but it was closed AFTER, which is not the same as run.
#
# =========================================================================================
# PART B — THE POSITIVE CONTROL, AND WHAT ITS PASS ACTUALLY BUYS
# =========================================================================================
#
#   test b1_daemon_bounds_an_ai_that_completes_the_protocol_and_stays_alive ... ok
#   test result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 18 filtered out; finished in 6.20s
#
# The control asserts FIRST, before the observation it validates, so a pass means the scanner
# saw the real AI **alive, by `comm`, in the daemon's own process group, during the run**.
# Outcome 1 of the three named before the run.
#
# **What this settles:** the fixture demonstration TRANSFERS. `live_group_members` was shown
# to work against a child spawned by the test process with `.process_group(0)`; the open
# question was whether that says anything about a real daemon, a fork/exec'd child and a pgid
# inherited across `execve`. It does. The reasoning the closure rested on — that neither
# `launcher.rs` nor `execute.rs` calls `setsid`/`setpgid` — is now MEASURED rather than argued,
# and an empty `survivors_before_guard` is therefore a real observation about the daemon.
#
# **What it does NOT settle, unchanged:** the run observes CLEANUP, not REAPING. R29-05's
# `Unreaped` branch stays UNCONSTRUCTED. The binary is built with `insecure-test-seams`, so the
# logind gate is bypassable and nothing here certifies the production gate.
#
# **The honest shape of this evidence:** a passing control CONFIRMS an argument rather than
# overturning one, which is the weaker of the two outcomes on offer. The failure would have
# been worth more — it would have withdrawn a closure that has now been restated twice. That it
# passed is a fact about the code, not a vindication of how the claim was made.
#
# A benign transcript line was traced rather than waved off: `userdel: user 'db1stubborn3942'
# does not exist` is `Setup::new`'s UNCONDITIONAL pre-clean on a clean path. `leader_is_shell.rs`
# already guards that exact noise with an existence check and documents why (expected-error
# noise buries the real failures beside it); `b1_integration.rs` did not — class 48, the fix
# scoped to the file where it was noticed. Guarded now.
#
